Logging Rules
Workflow Invocation Logs

Workflow start and end logs should be configured from workflow settings as:

Level: Info
Only Invocation: Enabled
Successful Return: Enabled

Flag as Warning if workflow invocation logging is missing or inconsistent.

If this cannot be verified from repository files, place it under Manual Checks.

Workflow Start and End Logging

Workflow start and end logs must be configured from workflow settings, not manually added as Log Message activities.

Expected workflow settings:

Level: Info
Only Invocation: Enabled
Successful Return: Enabled

Manual start/end logs should not be used as a substitute for workflow invocation logging.

Flag as Warning if workflow start/end logs are manually added instead of using workflow settings.

Flag as Suggestion if both workflow settings and redundant manual start/end logs exist.

Use Manual Check if this cannot be verified from repository files.

Operational Logging

Important operational steps should have meaningful logs, especially:

Transaction start
Transaction end
Queue item retrieval
Business validation result
External system/API call
File read/write
Mail read/send
Exception handling
Retry decision
Add Queue Item
Database update
File move/upload
Integration response

Flag as Critical if transaction-level traceability is missing.

Flag as Warning if logs exist but are too generic.

Log Context

Logs should include process-relevant context when available:

processName
transactionId / trxId
queueItemReference
businessKey
environment
workflowName
stepName
exceptionType
exceptionMessage

Flag as Warning when logs do not provide enough context for support analysis.

Log Message Quality

Avoid vague logs such as:

Started
Done
Error occurred

Prefer descriptive logs such as:

Invoice validation started for trxId={trxId}
Queue item added with reference={reference}
Business exception thrown because invoice amount is invalid
Database update completed for invoiceId={invoiceId}

Flag vague logs as Suggestion or Warning depending on impact.

External Logging

If the project uses Splunk, Elastic, or another logging system, verify that key transaction and exception events are traceable.

Do not assume external logging is broken only because full runtime configuration is not visible.

Use Manual Check if log target configuration cannot be verified from repository files.
